# spa/.dockerignore
# Story 6.2: first per-service .dockerignore live in the repo. The SPA's
# Docker build context is `../spa` (set in compose/app.yml), so this file
# is the only ignore file consulted for the SPA image — the repo-root
# .dockerignore covers the BFF + RS root-context builds and does not
# apply here.
#
# Goal: keep the build context to ~5 MB. Without this, sending the SPA's
# live working tree (with node_modules + .angular cache) would push ~250 MB
# through the Docker daemon socket on every build.

# Dependencies — Stage 0 `deps` runs `npm ci` from a clean state, so the
# host's node_modules is wasted bandwidth.
node_modules

# Build output — Stage 1 `build` regenerates this; never copy stale local
# builds in.
dist

# Test / cache artefacts
coverage
.angular

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*

# VCS — build context already excludes the parent .git, but keep the safety
# net for nested .git directories or accidental copies.
.git
.gitignore

# IDE / editor / OS cruft
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store

# Story 6.2 code-review P3: defense-in-depth against secret leakage. Angular
# projects don't normally carry `.env`-style files but if a developer creates
# `spa/.env.local` (common Angular pattern from other ecosystems), it would
# otherwise be copied into the build context and baked into the build stage's
# filesystem layer.
.env
.env.*

# Story 6.2 code-review P4: Vitest specs are not bundled into the SSR
# output (Angular's `@angular/build:application` only includes files
# reachable from the build entry points). Excluding them from the build
# context is purely a context-size optimization; nothing in the build
# pipeline reads `.spec.ts` files. Matches both top-level and nested specs.
**/*.spec.ts
